WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.334
Pharmaceutical dependence.

00:00:02.284 --> 00:00:04.852
Canada can't make its own medicine.

00:00:04.852 --> 00:00:11.590
Approximately eighty percent of active pharmaceutical ingredients used in Canadian medicines come from China and India.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:16.102
Canada has virtually zero domestic API manufacturing capacity.

00:00:16.102 --> 00:00:21.636
During COVID-19, both China and India restricted pharmaceutical exports.

00:00:21.636 --> 00:00:23.829
Canada had no fallback.

00:00:23.829 --> 00:00:32.988
The PMPRB, the regulator designed to control drug prices, has failed to bring Canadian prices below OECD average despite years of reform attempts.

00:00:32.988 --> 00:00:36.534
Pharmaceutical lobbying is among the highest in Canada.

00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:46.761
The result: Canadians pay above OECD average for medicines they cannot make, sourced from countries that can cut supply at will, regulated by a captured agency that cannot control prices.

00:00:46.761 --> 00:00:49.499
This is healthcare sovereignty surrendered.

00:00:49.499 --> 00:00:52.522
Three dimensions of pharmaceutical dependence.

00:00:52.522 --> 00:00:55.704
Pharmaceutical dependence and the MAID pipeline.

00:00:55.704 --> 00:01:00.306
Pharmaceutical dependence intersects with the MAID pipeline at multiple points.

00:01:00.306 --> 00:01:05.636
Patients who cannot afford medication because pharmacare is incomplete see their conditions worsen.

00:01:05.636 --> 00:01:12.397
Patients whose medications face supply disruptions because APIs are imported from China experience treatment gaps.

00:01:12.397 --> 00:01:19.988
Patients in provinces with the highest doctor shortages — documented in the doctor shortage analysis — face the longest waits for prescriptions.

00:01:19.988 --> 00:01:25.829
Each pharmaceutical failure contributes to the health deterioration that manufactures MAID eligibility.

00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:30.715
The system that cannot provide medicine is the system that offers death as an alternative.

00:01:30.715 --> 00:01:38.840
And the pharmaceutical industry that lobbies against price controls and pharmacare expansion profits from both the high prices and the system failures they produce.
